GNU/Linux Configuration
=======================

   GNU/Linux has very flexible kernel configuration features.  If you
use GNU/Linux, make it sure that the current kernel configuration is
what you want.  Zebra will run with any kernel configuration but some
recommendations do exist.

CONFIG_NETLINK
     Kernel/User netlink socket.  This is a brand new feature which
     enables an advanced interface between the Linux kernel and Zebra
     (*note Linux Netlink::.).

CONFIG_RTNETLINK
     Routing messages.  This makes it possible to receive netlink
     routing messages.  If you specify this option, `zebra' can detect
     routing information updates directly from the kernel (*note Kernel
     Interface::.).

CONFIG_IP_MULTICAST
     IP: multicasting.  This option should be specified when you use
     `ripd' or `ospfd' because these protocols use multicast.

   IPv6 supported is added from GNU/Linux kernel version 2.2.  If you
try to use the Zebra IPv6 feature on a GNU/Linux kernel, please make
sure the below library has been installed.

`inet6-apps'
     `inet6-apps' package includes basic IPv6 related libraries such as
     `inet_ntop' and `inet_pton'.  Some basic IPv6 programs such as
     `ping', `ftp', `inetd' are also included. `inet-apps' can be found
     at `ftp://ftp.inner.net/pub/ipv6/'.

`net-tools'
     `net-tools' provides an IPv6 enabled interface and routing utility.
     It contains `ifconfig', `route', `netstat' and other tools.
     `net-tools' can be found at `ftp://somewhere/'.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Configure Options,  Next: VTY Port Setup,  Prev: GNU/Linux Configuration,  Up: Installation

Configure Options
=================

   Normally the Zebra configure scripts detect host configurations
automatically.  For manual configuration, there are several configure
options.  You can turn off IPv6 support or disable the making of some
daemons using configure options.

`--enable-guile'
     Turn on compilation of the zebra-guile interpreter.  You will need
     the guile library to make this.

`--disable-ipv6'
     Turn off IPv6 related features and daemons.

`--disable-zebra'
     Do not build zebra daemon.

`--disable-bgpd'
     Do not build bgpd.

`--disable-ripd'
     Do not build ripd.

`--disable-ripngd'
     Do not build ripngd.

`--disable-ospfd'
     Do not build ospfd.

`--disable-ospf6d'
     Do not build ospf6d.

`--disable-pthread'
     Do not use pthread features.  Default is to disable this feature
     so you don't need to specify it.

   You can specify any of the above options to the configure script.
Configuration and compilation is very simple.  You run `configure' and
then `make' Zebra:

     % ./configure --disable-ipv6
     % make

   will create zebra and other beasts.  Installation is done by:

     % make install

   This will install all of the Zebra software under PREFIX/sbin.
Sample configuration installed under PREFIX/etc.  If you want to change
the installation directory please specify the following options to the
configure script.

`--prefix=PREFIX'
     Install architecture-independent files in PREFIX [/usr/local].

`--sysconfdir=DIR'
     Read-only sample configuration file in DIR [PREFIX/etc].


File: zebra.info,  Node: VTY Port Setup,  Prev: Configure Options,  Up: Installation

VTY Port Setup
==============

   Zebra daemon's have their own terminal interfaces each is called a
VTY.  So after the Zebra beasts are installed you have to setup each
program's port number for connecting to them.  Please add the following
entries to `/etc/services'.

     zebrasrv      2600/tcp		  # zebra service
     zebra         2601/tcp		  # zebra vty
     ripd          2602/tcp		  # RIPd vty
     ripngd        2603/tcp		  # RIPngd vty
     ospfd         2604/tcp		  # OSPFd vty
     bgpd          2605/tcp		  # BGPd vty
     ospf6d        2606/tcp		  # OSPF6d vty

   If you use a FreeBSD newer than 2.2.8, the above entry is already
added to `/etc/services'.  So there is no need to add it.


